.TH smi-postgresql-create-db-sql "1" "November 2026" "SMI Toolset" "SMI Base Command"
.SH NAME
smi-postgresql-create-db-sql \- Print the SQL that creates a PostgreSQL database with an application user and a Liquibase user
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smi-postgresql-create-db-sql
.I NAME
.B live|test
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Prints, on standard output, the SQL that creates the database
.IB NAME _ ENVIRONMENT
and two users for it, each with a new random password from
.BR smi-password-gen (1),
unless the password is given in the environment.
Nothing is executed: the output is meant for
.BR psql (1)
as a superuser, and it uses the
.B \econnect
command of
.BR psql ,
because the schema privileges have to be set inside the new database.
.TP
.IB ENVIRONMENT NAME liquibase
The Liquibase user. It owns the database and its
.I public
schema, so it creates, changes and drops tables and other objects, and changes data, through
.BR smi-liquibase (1).
.TP
.IB ENVIRONMENT NAME
The application user, for the service that uses the database. It can connect, read and change
rows with
.BR SELECT ,
.BR INSERT ,
.B UPDATE
and
.BR DELETE ,
use sequences with
.B nextval
and
.BR currval ,
and call the functions the Liquibase user creates. It can not create, change or drop any object,
truncate a table, set a sequence, create temporary tables or grant anything to anyone.
.PP
These privileges reach the tables, sequences and functions the Liquibase user creates later,
through default privileges, so no grant is needed after a migration. Other roles of the server
can not connect to the database, and functions created there are not executable by
.B PUBLIC .
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I NAME
Lower case letters, digits and underscores, not starting with a digit, as an unquoted PostgreSQL
identifier is.
.TP
.B live|test
The environment, part of the database name and of both user names, so one server can hold both
without a clash.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SMI_DB_APP_PASSWORD
The password of the application user. Unset or empty, a random one is generated.
.TP
.B SMI_DB_LIQUIBASE_PASSWORD
The password of the Liquibase user. Unset or empty, a random one is generated.
.PP
A given password is written into the SQL as a string literal with its single quotes doubled, so
any character is safe.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The SQL was printed.
.TP
.B 1
Wrong number of arguments, an invalid name or an unknown environment.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Look at the SQL, keep it for the passwords, and apply it:
.RS
.nf
smi-postgresql-create-db-sql shop test > shop_test.sql
sudo -u postgres psql -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -f shop_test.sql
.fi
.RE
.PP
This creates the database
.IR shop_test ,
the application user
.I testshop
and the Liquibase user
.IR testshopliquibase .
The passwords are in the file only, so it is kept somewhere safe or removed once they are stored.
.PP
Then apply the changelogs as the Liquibase user:
.RS
.nf
SMI_DB_NAME=shop_test SMI_DB_USER=testshopliquibase smi-liquibase update
.fi
.RE
.PP
Keep a password the service already has, for example when the database is created again on
another server:
.RS
.nf
SMI_DB_APP_PASSWORD='secret from the vault' smi-postgresql-create-db-sql shop live > shop_live.sql
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR psql (1),
.BR smi-liquibase (1),
.BR smi-password-gen (1)
.SH AUTHOR
setmy.info
